(area "agents" :when "the work involves another agent: starting, messaging, checking on, saving or reusing a sub-agent, or splitting jobs to run in parallel")

(memory "agents/names" :when "the agent must refer to a spawned agent by name, list the agents this session started, or tell the user when a reply will arrive")
Names are unique in session. spawn refuses one taken, finished or not, and "parent" is taken: sub-agents see you as <system-event source="agent:parent">. spawn and delegate print the name they return. agent-wait, agent-state, agent-take and agent-cancel take it.
(spawned) lists every name this session spawned or delegated. (agents) lists saved definitions only.
Not waited? Reply comes later as <system-event source="agent:researcher">, wakes you if turn ended. Agent keeps its chat between sends. Spawned names survive restart in your image.

(memory "agents/named" :when "a sub-agent must keep its own chat across messages, or the agent is about to send more work to, check on, or stop an agent already running")
Named agent, kept chat across sends, order always:
1. (agents) lists saved names. (agent-definition "researcher") reads one.
   Saved one fits? Spawn it by name. Never save second agent for same job.
2. None fits? Spawn temporary one with :prompt. Nothing saved. :prompt is first
   task, run at once, not persona: say what to do and what to reply.
3. Temporary one did job well, same job will come again? (agent-keep "scratch")
   saves it. Else let it go.

  (spawn "researcher" :repl :fresh)      ; prints "spawned researcher"; saved definition, new image, new chat; idle till send
  (spawn "scratch" :prompt "..." :repl :fork :chat :fork) ; temporary, runs prompt now; copy of your image and chat
  (send :to "researcher" "find X" :max-steps 10) ; returns "researcher" at once
  (agent-state "researcher")             ; last send: :idle :pending :held :done :failed :cancelled :lost
  (agent-wait "researcher")              ; wait on last send, return reply
  (agent-cancel "researcher")            ; stop it, its last send queued or running, and all under it

agent-wait and agent-state see last send only. Earlier send's reply comes as
system event. Need each reply? agent-wait before next send.

Spawn options: :repl :fresh gives new image, :fork copy of this one as it
stands. :chat :fresh starts with no conversation; :chat :fork with a copy of
this chat so far. Prefer :chat :fresh; :chat :fork only to continue this exact
work. :budget caps its steps. :permissions holds permission forms as text,
only tightens workspace rules. Asks from sub-agents reach user in your chat.
User @sub-agents/researcher = saved definition "researcher": spawn it, send it
the ask.

(memory "agents/saved" :when "the agent is about to save an agent definition, keep a temporary agent for reuse, or delete a saved one")
defagent and agent-keep save sub-agents/<name>.ptc; agent-forget deletes it.
:prompt on saved name refused. Saved name spawns without :prompt.

  (defagent "reader" :prompt "..." :permissions "(permission/deny \"fs/*\")" :budget 40)

:roster names the agents it may spawn in turn. :budget caps its steps.
agent-forget returns t, or nil if none was saved.
An agent already spawned from a deleted definition keeps running.

(memory "agents/take" :when "the agent needs a value a sub-agent produced copied into this session, or wants to see what an agent exports")
Sub-agent may export values. Reply then ends with exports: name, what it
holds, shape. Want one? Copy it into your image by agent name, bind it yourself:
  (setq rows (agent-take "scratch" 'prices))
  (setq id (agent-take "delegate-1" 'id))
Reply shows (agent-take "agent" 'name): put real agent name for "agent".
Value is copied as it stands then. Function taken keeps captured variables;
globals it names resolve in your image.

(memory "agents/quietly" :when "the agent is about to wrap a form in quietly, or run a form without being woken for its result")
Message and its result stay in chat; read them next turn.

(memory "agents/delegate" :when "a job is a good fit for a fresh agent, or several independent jobs could run in parallel")
One-off job: (delegate "brief") spawns fresh temporary agent, sends brief,
prints "delegated to delegate-1", returns "delegate-1" at once. agent-wait it, or reply comes as system event.
Delegate replies result, evidence, what not checked.
  (setq a (delegate "read A, reply path and line")) (setq b (delegate "read B"))
  (list (agent-wait a) (agent-wait b))   ; parallel: delegate all first, wait after
